# About Us — Panificio Vitale (Sicilian bakery, est. 1962)

The brief doesn't name the bakery, so I've invented one concrete anchor to design against: **Panificio Vitale**, a bread-and-pastry shop two streets off Piazza Ballarò in Palermo, opened in 1962 by Salvatore and Rosa Vitale, now run by their kids and grandkids. Swap the name/address if you have a real one — the structure and voice hold either way.

## Design plan

**Color** — Sicily's bakery world isn't cream-and-terracotta; it's the inside of a pastry case: marzipan, glazed fruit, majolica tile.
- `#F7EFD9` Semolina — base background, the color of flour, not café cream
- `#FFFDF7` Marble — panel/card surfaces, cool against the warmer base
- `#4B7C67` Pistachio — primary accent, the marzipan coating on a cassata
- `#B23A48` Cherry Glacé — secondary accent, candied cherry, used sparingly for emphasis
- `#C9762E` Candied Peel — tertiary warm accent, rooftop/citrus-peel orange, used least of all
- `#1E2B24` Ink Pine — body text; a green-black tied to the palette instead of flat #111

**Type** — One warm serif with real personality, one plain sans for legibility. No caps, no tracked-out labels.
- **Fraunces** (italic + upright) for the shop-sign headline and the pull quote — its soft, slightly old-style curves read as hand-painted signage, not corporate display type
- **Archivo** for body copy, times, and addresses — grounded, no personality competing with Fraunces

**Layout** — Think of the page as walking past the shop: awning outside, counter and case inside, a ticket in your hand on the way out. Left-aligned throughout (this is a story, not a poster); hero copy centered only because that's how a shop sign is centered over a door.

```
+----------------------------------------------+
| Vitale                          Menu   Visit  |
|                                                |
|              Panificio Vitale                 |
|                 dal 1962                      |
|     in Ballarò, one of the oldest markets     |
|            in Palermo                         |
+~~~scalloped awning edge~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~+
| Salvatore & Rosa, 1962           "She always  |
| (family story, two columns,       said you    |
|  narrow measure)                  could hear  |
|                                    when the   |
|                                    dough was  |
|                                    ready."     |
+------------------------------------------------+
| What's always in the case                    |
| Cannoli ............ shells fried to order    |
| Cassata ............ made weekly, her recipe  |
| Brioche col tuppo .. dunked in granita        |
| ...                                            |
+------------------------------------------------+
| 4:30  Dough goes in                           |
| 6:00  Ovens up, case filled                    |
| 7:30  Doors open                               |
| ...  (real clock-time sequence, left rail)     |
+------------------------------------------------+
|   [ ticket-style card: address / hours / tel ] |
+------------------------------------------------+
| Panificio Vitale — family-run since 1962      |
+------------------------------------------------+
```

**Principles**
1. The awning scallop and the ticket card are the two structural devices — both come from the shop itself, not decoration borrowed from a template.
2. One motion moment: the sign settles into place on load, nothing else moves on its own.
3. The daily-rhythm section is the only place with a numbered/ordered device, because it's the only genuinely sequential content.
4. No caps-locked eyebrows, no middle-dot metadata, no arrows on links — the voice is a family talking about their own shop, not a brand deck.

### Self-critique against the generic default

Running the same brief through a generic pass would likely produce: cream background, terracotta accent, a big "1962" in giant numerals with "years of tradition" underneath, three feature cards with soft shadows for the pastries, and an ALL-CAPS "OUR STORY" eyebrow. I changed:
- **Palette** from cream/terracotta to marzipan/pistachio/cherry-glacé — pulled from the cassata itself, not the AI-default warm neutral.
- **Hero treatment** from "big number, small label" (1962) to a shop-sign composition with the awning scallop, because the number alone tells you nothing about *this* bakery.
- **"What we bake"** from identical rounded cards to a menu-board list, because a price-list rhythm is how a real bakery actually displays this information, and cards would flatten six very different pastries into one shape.
- **Sequence markers** — I almost defaulted to 01/02/03 for the daily rhythm, then swapped to real clock times, which is both more accurate and more specific to a bakery's day.
- **Copy** — no "Est. 1962" badge language; the founding date is stated once, plainly, in the family's own telling.

