# Plan

## Grounding
Subject: **Pasticceria Bonomo**, a family-run pastry shop in the Kalsa quarter of Palermo, open since 1962. Audience: people who've heard about the shop and want the story before they walk in — regulars' grandchildren, tourists who found it through a friend, the neighborhood. Job of the page: make the shop's specific history and daily rhythm legible — who's behind the counter, what's actually in the case, when to show up.

## Color
- `#E7DFC9` stucco — limewashed plaster, the shop's actual wall color. Warmer and grayer than the AI-default cream.
- `#221D16` ink — near-black warm brown, not a tinted `#111`.
- `#24518E` cobalt — majolica blue, the dominant color of Caltagirone tile work. Primary accent, replacing the expected terracotta.
- `#D9A22B` ochre — sun/lemon, used only in the tile frieze and small marks, never as body text (fails contrast on stucco).
- `#6B7F4A` olive — secondary tile color.
- `#A8432B` oxide — brick red from *carretto siciliano* cart painting, used sparingly for one citation mark.

## Type
- **Bodoni Moda** (display) — an Italian typeface, its high-contrast strokes are what's stamped on actual Italian pastry-shop packaging and liquor labels. Used for the name and headings, and italic for one handwritten-feeling tagline.
- **Archivo** (body/labels) — a plain grotesque, functioning like a price board: legible, unfussy, no personality competing with Bodoni.

## Layout
Single-column scroll, left-aligned body copy (measure ≤62ch), hero centered — shop signage over a door is symmetric, so the one place centering earns its keep.

```
┌────────────────────────────┐
│      Pasticceria Bonomo     │  hero, centered
│      Palermo, since 1962    │
│   "Cannoli filled while..." │
│  ▚▞▚▞ tile frieze ▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞  │  ← homage to the ceramic-
└────────────────────────────┘    tiled steps of Caltagirone
│ Three generations, one      │
│ counter                      │
│ 1962 │ Salvatore & Rosa      │  ledger, left-aligned,
│ 1989 │ Turi & Ninfa          │  because it IS a real
│ today│ Chiara & Dario        │  chronological sequence
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ What hasn't changed  | quote │  two-column on desktop,
│ text text text       | from  │  stacked on mobile
│ text text text       | note- │
│                       | book  │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ In the case today            │  price-board list with
│ Cannolo ..... filled to order│  dot leaders — the actual
│ Cassata ..... Sundays        │  vernacular of a bakery case
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ Find us       | Come before   │
│ address/hours | nine for the  │
│               | sfincione…    │
└────────────────────────────┘
```

## Principles
- One motion moment: the hero name, tagline, and tile frieze arrive in a single staggered sequence on load; nothing else animates on scroll or hover beyond a quiet underline.
- The tile frieze is a structural device (a real Caltagirone step-tile motif), not a generic gradient wash.
- The ledger's years are real chronology, not decorative numbering.
- No eyebrow labels, no all-caps, no middle-dot meta strings, no arrows on links.

## Review against the brief
My first instinct matched the described AI default almost exactly — cream background, terracotta accent, single dramatic serif. I revised: background moved from cream toward an actual limewash-plaster tone; the accent moved from terracotta to cobalt (majolica blue), which is just as native to Sicily but isn't the flagged Claude-tell color; I paired the display serif with a plain grotesque instead of leaving it to carry the whole page alone; the "three generations" sequence keeps numbering because it's a genuine timeline, but nothing else on the page gets a 01/02/03 treatment; the case list uses dot leaders because that's literally how a bakery posts its case, not as decoration. I dropped an early idea to open with an ALL-CAPS "ESTABLISHED 1962" plaque — that's the tracked-out eyebrow tell — and replaced it with a plain sentence-case line instead.

