# Design Plan — Panificio Greco, "About Us"

**Subject:** A real, still-operating family bakery in Ortigia (Siracusa), Sicily, baking since 1962 — now run by the third generation. Audience: people who might actually walk in — locals, tourists staying in Ortigia, people searching "bakery near me." Job of the page: make the shop feel worth finding in person, and explain why it doesn't take online orders.

### Color
Sicilian identity here comes from Caltagirone majolica ceramics (sold in shops all over Ortigia) rather than the generic warm-cream/terracotta palette — so I'm leading with tile blue and ochre instead.

| Token | Hex | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `--plaster` | `#E7DEC9` | Page background — whitewashed plaster, warmer/greyer than the usual AI cream |
| `--paper` | `#F6F1E4` | Card/panel surfaces — bakery wrapping paper |
| `--charcoal` | `#2B2117` | Body text — oven soot, not pure/tinted black |
| `--cobalt` | `#1F4E79` | Primary accent — majolica blue |
| `--saffron` | `#CE9A2E` | Secondary accent — glaze yellow |
| `--brick` | `#A34A2A` | Tertiary accent, used sparingly — distinct from the common `#D97757` |

### Type
Two families, clearly distinct roles:
- **Piazzolla** (serif, display) — headlines only. Has old-style figures and a slightly formal, printed-menu character that fits a 1962 shopfront rather than a startup.
- **Archivo** (sans, body/UI) — everything else. Squarish, slightly blunt letterforms; reads plainly at small sizes.

No small caps, no tracked-out labels, no monospace for data.

### Layout
```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Panificio Greco            [tile medal-│
│  A wood-fired bakery in      lion, CSS  │
│  Ortigia, Sicily, since      conic       │
│  1962.                       pattern]    │
├──[thin tile-stripe divider]──────────────┤
│  Three generations, one oven             │
│  ●1962 Salvatore opens the oven ...      │
│  ●1978 Maria adds the pastry counter ... │
│  ●2005 Nino takes over ...               │
│  ●Today  still no order form ...         │
├───────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  What's in the case today                │
│  Pane nero ................ €4           │
│  Mafalda al sesamo ......... €2          │
│  Cannolo siciliano ......... €3          │
│  Cassata (Sunday only) ..... €5          │
│  Granita e brioche ......... €4          │
├──[thin tile-stripe divider]──────────────┤
│  Visit us — address, hours, no-order note│
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
Left-aligned throughout (matches Italian shop-sign/menu vernacular, keeps line lengths under 80 characters); the timeline is the one place a vertical spine with dated markers is used, because the content is genuinely chronological — not decorative numbering.

### Principles
- One bold move: the majolica tile medallion in the hero and the tile-stripe dividers — everything else stays quiet paper/plaster.
- Real chronology earns the timeline dots; nothing else gets a numbered marker.
- Copy explains, in the shop's own voice, why there's no "order online" button — that's the actual point of an about page for a place like this.

**Review against brief:** My first instinct was cream background + serif headline + terracotta accent — that's the generic AI bakery/heritage-brand default. I replaced it with the majolica blue/saffron/brick trio and a greyer plaster background, and swapped the decorative element from a gradient wash to an actual Sicilian ceramic pattern. I also dropped the tracked-out eyebrow label I'd normally reach for ("EST. 1962") and folded the date into a plain sentence instead.

