# Plan

**Subject.** Pasticceria Ferrante, Via Cavour 14, Noto, Sicily — a wood-oven bakery a family has run since 1962, now on its third generation. Audience: locals and visitors deciding whether to trust and visit a shop they've never been to. The page's job is to make the shop feel lived-in and specific, not "artisan bakery" in the abstract.

**First instinct, and why I dropped it.** My default reflex for "family bakery since 1962" would be: cream paper background, a high-contrast serif display for the name, a single muted terracotta accent on buttons and an underline, thin hairline rules between sections, and an "EST. 1962" eyebrow label above the headline. That's traits 1, 3, and 5 stacked on top of each other — the generic answer to almost any heritage-food brief, Sicilian or not.

**Revised after review:** swapped the cream/terracotta/serif-display combo for a palette pulled from actual Sicilian majolica tiles and the shop's own ingredients (cobalt, blood orange, lemon, pistachio) on a warm limewash-plaster ground; replaced the literary high-contrast serif with a slab "sign-painted" display face set inside an awning shape as the hero's one bold gesture; and cut the "EST. 1962" eyebrow label, folding the date into the sign copy itself ("dal 1962") — because those were the parts that would've looked identical on a bakery brief in Rome, Tokyo, or nowhere in particular.

## Color
- Majolica cobalt `#1E4870` — the awning, the sign
- Blood orange `#D5451C` — links, the featured case item
- Lemon `#F0AE0C` — small marks, hover states
- Pistachio `#6E8B3D` — timeline markers, borders
- Plaster `#E8D8AE` — page background (limewashed courtyard wall, not paper)
- Ink `#241F16` — body text

## Type
- **Bevan** (slab, condensed, sign-painted character) — wordmark, section headings
- **Vollkorn** (warm old-style serif, generous line-height) — body copy, timeline, case descriptions
Two families, clearly distinct roles: one is drawn, one is read.

## Layout
Not cards, not a broadsheet — a bakery counter, read top to bottom the way you'd walk past a display case.

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ░░ scalloped cobalt awning ░░           │
│    PASTICCERIA FERRANTE   dal 1962       │
│    Noto, Sicilia                         │
├───── tile-stripe divider (used once) ────┤
│  La nostra storia          "Il pane..."  │
│  (text, ≤62ch)             — Salvatore   │
├───────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Tre generazioni                         │
│  ♦ 1962  Salvatore & Concetta            │
│  ♦ 1988  Rosaria                         │
│  ♦ 2015  Dario & Elena                   │
├───────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Nella vetrina (irregular case grid)     │
│  [ cannoli, big ] [ cassatelle ]         │
│  [ mandorla ] [ granita ]                │
│  [        torrone, wide banner       ]   │
├───── tile-stripe divider (used once) ────┤
│  Vieni a trovarci — address / hours      │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
Left-aligned throughout, body text capped near 62 characters. The three generations get dated markers because they're genuinely chronological; nothing else in the page is numbered.

## Principles
1. Color and pattern come from the tiles and ingredients themselves — cobalt, citrus, pistachio — not one safe "craft" accent.
2. One bold gesture (the awning sign); everything below it is quiet and text-led.
3. Dates live inside sentences, not as eyebrow labels.
4. The display case looks like a case — uneven, one thing bigger because it's the thing people actually come for.

