**Assumptions** (open to redirection): No name, city, or specific offerings were given, so I've invented concrete ones to design against — a bakery called **Panificio Vitale**, on a side street off the Ballarò market in Palermo, founded in 1962 by Turi and Agata Vitale and now run by their grandchildren Rosa and Nino. Audience: locals and visitors who want to know who's actually behind the counter, not a sales pitch. Job of the page: make the family and the sixty-year routine feel specific and real. Swap in the real name/city/family details and the design holds.

---

## Design plan

**Color** — drawn from Sicilian majolica tile-work and the citrus/oven materials of the bakery itself, not a bakery-generic cream-and-terracotta:

| Name | Hex | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Intonaco | `#EDE6D6` | limewashed-plaster base, light sections |
| Mare | `#1B4B5A` | majolica sea-blue, hero + footer ground |
| Limone | `#E8B93A` | citrus accent, hero highlights |
| Melanzana | `#3D2733` | aubergine, dividers/hairlines |
| Forno | `#A8462F` | brick-oven rust, links/small accents |
| Carbone | `#241F1C` | warm ink, body text on light ground |

**Type** — Fraunces (a warm, slightly old-style serif with real character in its ink-traps) for display, set large and confident; Archivo (a sturdy grotesque with a sign-painted feel) for body and labels. Two clearly distinct roles, no default pairing.

- H1: `clamp(2.75rem, 6vw, 5rem)` Fraunces 600, line-height 1.05
- H2: `clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem)` Fraunces 600
- Body: 17px/1.6 Archivo, max 34em (~68 characters) measure
- Small/meta: 14px Archivo, sentence case (no all-caps)

**Layout** — the shopfront, not the SaaS grid:

```
HERO   [ Mare blue, full-bleed ]
       Headline, left-aligned      ⌒⌒⌒  <- arch motif, right
       Subhead                     ⌒⌒⌒     (Norman-Arab arch,
       small plaque, bottom-left            Palermo's own)

STORY  Our story
       1962 ── text
       1971 ── text          (a real chronology, so year
       1985 ── text           markers earn their place)
       Today ── text

MENU   What we bake
       [ dish — description ]  [ dish — description ]
       ————————————————————    ———————————————————— (two-col list,
       [ dish — description ]  [ dish — description ]  hairline rules)

FAMILY  The family
       [arch] Turi   [arch] Agata   [arch] Salvo   [arch] Rosa/Nino

VISIT  Address, hours — Mare footer band
```

Left-aligned throughout (a market stall reads left to right, not centered like a poster); serif headlines get slightly more line-height than the sans body per the brief's own material — bread and tile, not glossy food photography.

**Principles**
1. The arch — Palermo's Norman-Arab window shape — is the one motif, reused small (family frames) and large (hero), so it reads as a signature rather than decoration.
2. The timeline is a real family chronology, not a generic 3-step process.
3. One motion moment: the hero looks like a shutter rolling up at opening time. Nothing else moves on scroll.
4. Copy is specific and first-person-adjacent (dish names, real streets, real hours) — no marketing verbs.

**Review against the brief:** my first instinct would have been cream background, serif headline, single terracotta accent — trait #1 from the cliché list, and an easy trap for "bakery." I moved the dominant hero color to majolica blue (an actual Sicilian material reference) and made rust/lemon secondary accents instead of the lead color. I also dropped an initial idea to number the family members 01–04 like a process — they aren't a sequence, so I kept the timeline numbered (year-based, since that *is* chronological) and made the family section a plain row instead.

