**Assumptions** — The brief doesn't name the shop, its town, or what it actually bakes, so I've invented concrete, plausible specifics to design against: **Panificio e Pasticceria Vella**, in Ortigia (the old island quarter of Syracuse, Sicily), founded 1962 by Salvatore and Rosa Vella, now run by their granddaughter Giusi — a bread-and-pastry counter known for sesame semolina bread, cannoli, cassata, and pasta di mandorla. Happy to swap in the real shop's name, town, or specialties if you share them.

---

## Design plan

**Color** — Sicily's visual signature isn't terracotta-and-cream trattoria decor, it's Caltagirone majolica ceramics: cobalt tilework against whitewashed plaster, with sun-yellow and pistachio glazes. That's the base, with a warm near-black standing in for ink rather than cold gray-black.

- `--plaster` `#EDE3D0` — warm plaster wall, base background
- `--majolica` `#1F5C8B` — ceramic cobalt, primary accent (nav, links, footer)
- `--sun` `#E8A93B` — glaze yellow, decoration only (fails contrast as text)
- `--pistachio` `#7A8B4F` — Bronte pistachio green, minor accent
- `--terracotta` `#B5502D` — roof-tile red, used sparingly, never as the hero color
- `--espresso` `#2E2016` — warm near-black for body text

**Type** — Bodoni is an Italian typeface (Giambattista Bodoni, Parma), so a display cut of it for headlines is a grounded, not decorative, choice — high-contrast, slightly theatrical, right for old shop signage. Paired with Work Sans for body and UI, a plain workhorse that won't compete.

- **Bodoni Moda** — h1/h2, pull-quote, timeline years, italic used for warmth (not single-word emphasis)
- **Work Sans** — body copy, nav, labels, captions

**Layout** — Mediterranean asymmetry over a rigid centered grid; left-aligned body copy under 65ch; one recurring structural motif — the Norman-Arab-Sicilian arch — used as an image frame and echoed as a tile-trim strip, rather than a decorative flourish repeated everywhere.

```
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| Vella                          Our story  What we bake  Visit |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|  The oven has stayed lit          .-""""-.                   |
|  since 1962.                     /  loaves \   (arch frame,  |
|  Three generations of the       |  sesame   |   sesame-seed  |
|  Vella family have kept...       \  crust  /    texture)     |
|  Via delle Vergini 14, Ortigia.   '--tile--'                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| Our story  (real timeline — 3 generations, so dates earn      |
|  1962 |— Salvatore and Rosa open the doors...                 |
|  1978 |— Pino takes over the ovens...                         |
|  2005 |— Giusi returns from Catania...                        |
| Today |— Giusi runs the counter...                            |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| What we bake — shelf of 4 items, hairline dividers, no cards  |
| Cannoli siciliani   | Cassata siciliana                       |
| Pasta di mandorla   | Pane con la giuggiulena                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| "Bread doesn't lie..."  — Giusi Vella          (pull-quote)   |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| Visit us — deep majolica-blue footer, hours, address, tile    |
|  trim strip echoing the hero arch                             |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
```

**Principles**
- The Caltagirone-ceramic palette and Norman-Sicilian arch are the identity — not a red/white/green Italian-flag cliché, not a script "trattoria" font.
- Spend the one bold move on the arch motif (hero frame + footer trim); everything else — spacing, section rhythm, typography — stays quiet and disciplined.
- The generational timeline uses real dates because the content is genuinely chronological, not a stylistic numbering device.
- No rounded-card kit, no drop shadows, no gradient decoration, no ALL-CAPS eyebrows, no middot/em-dash meta labels, no arrows on CTAs.

**Review against the brief** — My first instinct would've reached for cream-background-plus-terracotta-serif "cozy Italian bakery" defaults; I moved the dominant identity to majolica blue/yellow and demoted terracotta to a minor accent so it doesn't read as the generic AI bakery page. I also caught myself about to add an ALL-CAPS "OUR STORY" eyebrow above the timeline and a kicker line repeating the shop name above the hero — cut both; the wordmark in the header already carries that job, and a heading doesn't need a label announcing what it is.

