**Assumption:** the brief doesn't name the bakery, so I invented one to design against — **Pasticceria Ferrante**, three generations in Ortigia, the old island quarter of Siracusa, opened in 1962. Swap in the real name, place and family story once you confirm, and the rest of the structure carries over directly.

## Design plan

**Color** — Sicily's visual signature isn't cream-and-terracotta, it's majolica ceramic (Caltagirone tile) and citrus. Building from that instead of the usual bakery palette:
- `--semolina` `#F3E9D2` — flour-dusted paper, the base
- `--paper` `#FBF6EA` — lighter panel tone for content blocks
- `--ink` `#1F4E5F` — deep ceramic blue, used for display type and the tile frieze
- `--saffron` `#E3A72F` — citrus/saffron accent, used sparingly
- `--pistachio` `#7C8C3E` — Bronte pistachio green, smallest accent, used once or twice
- `--char` `#2B2420` — warm near-black for body text

**Type** — Fraunces (display, soft old-style terminals — reads like hand-painted pastry-box lettering) for headlines and the family name, set big and unapologetic. Archivo (grotesque sans) for body copy, nav and the specialties list, so the two do clearly different jobs rather than blurring together.

**Layout** — Centered, sign-like hero framed top and bottom by a thin majolica-tile checker strip (the only place that motif appears). Below it, left-aligned reading columns capped near 65ch. The family history is a genuine three-generation timeline (1962 → 1988 → 2015), so a vertical line with year markers is earned here, not decorative. The specialties are a ledger-style list — name, one line of description, hairline rule — not a grid of rounded cards.

```
+----------------------------------------------------+
| ▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞  (tile frieze, one use only)     |
|                                                      |
|              Pasticceria Ferrante                   |
|         The oven has not gone cold since 1962       |
|                     (1962 seal)                     |
| ▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞▚▞                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------+
| Our story                                           |
|  1962 ●— Salvatore & Concetta light the oven...     |
|         |                                            |
|  1988 ●— Turi adds the pastry case...               |
|         |                                            |
|  2015 ●— Mimmo comes home from Palermo...           |
+----------------------------------------------------+
| What we bake                                        |
|  Cannolo ........................................   |
|  Cassata siciliana ..............................   |
|  Pasta di mandorla ..............................   |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|  Visit us                    [line-art skyline]     |
|  Via della Giudecca 14                              |
|  Tue–Sun 6:30–14:00, 16:30–20:00                    |
+----------------------------------------------------+
```

**Principles** — one bold move (the shop-sign hero + tile frieze), everything else quiet paper and ledger lines. The 1962 seal is a real object (a bakery-box stamp), not a stat card. No copy sells "passion" or "love" — it states what actually happens each morning.

## Review against the brief

My first instinct was the default bakery page: cream background, terracotta accent, Playfair-style serif, three rounded feature cards with a soft shadow, an "ESTABLISHED 1962" eyebrow over the hero. I replaced the palette with majolica blue/saffron/pistachio because cream-and-terracotta is the generic AI bakery look, not a Sicilian one. I dropped the eyebrow label and the big-number-plus-gradient treatment in favor of the stamp seal, since a floating label reads as template chrome. The numbered timeline stayed, because the content really is a three-generation sequence — that's the one place numbering is earned. The specialties became a ledger list instead of cards, since six almost-identical rounded boxes with the same shadow was the SaaS default, not a bakery menu board.

