**Assumption:** Your brief didn't name the service, so I invented a placeholder brand, "Tapehouse," and its copy/pricing purely to ground the design in something concrete. Swap the name, prices, and feature text for your real product before shipping.

## Design plan

**Color** — `#201D1A` ink (warm charcoal, not pure black), `#2B2822` panel, `#332E24` panel-lit (the "recommended" channel), `#EDE7D8` paper (text), `#A79C87` paper-dim (muted text), `#E3A438` amber (VU-meter accent), `#7C8B6F` olive (secondary accent).

**Type** — Fraunces for the headline and tier names (warm, editorial, a little idiosyncratic — reads like liner notes, not a SaaS deck). IBM Plex Sans for body copy and UI text. IBM Plex Mono reserved *only* for the price digits, styled like a tape-counter readout — the one typographic accent on the page.

**Layout** — The pricing table *is* the hero: three tiers are rendered as mixing-console channel strips sitting side by side, since the subject is audio production. No separate hero banner above a separate pricing grid. Content is left-aligned within each strip (not centered, which is the SaaS-card default); the page column is narrow and left-set, echoing a spec sheet rather than a marketing deck. Below the strips, a plain comparison table (real hairline rules, since it's genuine tabular data) fills in detail. Flat panels, no drop shadows, no uniform pill radius — buttons get a small machined 4px corner instead.

**Principles** — One typographic moment (mono price digits), one accent color (amber) used sparingly for the recommended channel's lit edge, no eyebrow labels/all-caps/middot chrome, and copy written for an indie creator's actual concerns (owning your RSS feed, no lock-in, no surprise overage charges).

*Reviewed against defaults:* dropped an initial cream/terracotta instinct (too close to the common AI default) in favor of the darker analog-tape palette; dropped a "Most Popular" ribbon badge in favor of an in-voice sentence tied to the console metaphor; dropped decorative fader/level-meter bars I'd sketched in, since they added nothing the layout wasn't already saying — one accessory removed.

