**Assumption:** The brief didn't name the service, so I invented "Aircheck" — a real radio-industry term for a recording of a live broadcast, which fits a podcast-hosting product. Everything else below (tier names, prices, copy) is also invented placeholder content for you to edit.

## Design plan

**Color**
- `#1B222C` Signal Ink — dark navy-charcoal, hero/nav background
- `#EDE6D6` Tape Cream — warm paper tone for the pricing/body sections
- `#E8A33D` Dial Amber — primary accent, tuning-dial glow
- `#4C7A82` Static Teal — secondary accent
- `#2B2F36` Charcoal — body text on light backgrounds
- `#F7F5F0` Off-white — body text on dark backgrounds

**Type**
- Display: Bebas Neue — condensed, poster/signage weight, used for the headline, tier names and prices, evoking a station call-sign board.
- Body: Lora — a warm serif for descriptions and FAQ copy, giving the page an editorial, liner-notes voice. Two clearly distinct families, one job each.

**Layout**
Left-aligned throughout, not centered — the page reads more like a program log than a marketing deck.
```
[Aircheck]                    Help   Log in
Three ways
to get on
the air.
Every price includes your own RSS feed.
▁▂▄▇▆▃▅▇▄▂▁▃▅▇▆▄▂▁ (single-sweep waveform divider)

Billed monthly ⟷ Billed yearly

 GARAGE      │  ON AIR       │  SYNDICATE
 Free        │  $15/mo       │  $45/mo
 ...bullets  │  ...bullets   │  ...bullets
 [Start      │ ─amber rule─  │ [Start
  recording] │ [Go on air]   │  syndicating]
```
Three columns divided by hairline rules (one shared log sheet, not isolated cards); the middle tier is marked only by a rule under its price, no shadow or elevation.

**Principles**
- No rounded SaaS cards, no drop shadows, no gradient hero.
- One motion moment: the waveform sweeps in once on load; nothing else animates on scroll.
- Vocabulary stays consistent: plan name → button verb (On Air → "Go on air").

I checked this against the common AI-default traits (cream+terracotta+serif, near-black+neon, broadsheet columns, card-kit, all-caps eyebrows/em-dash labels/arrows) and adjusted the palette off terracotta toward gold, kept the dark section navy rather than near-black, and swapped cards for a log-sheet table.

