# Design Plan

**Subject / audience / job:** Panificio Lo Cascio, a family-run Sicilian bakery on Via Ballarò in Palermo, open since 1962 — now in its third generation. Audience is neighbourhood regulars and visitors to the Ballarò market wanting the human story behind the counter. Job: make the sixty-year family history feel warm, specific, and trustworthy, not like a generic "artisan bakery" template.

**Color**
- `--wheat: #F2E4C1` — durum semolina, page background
- `--cream: #FBF6E8` — lighter card surface
- `--ink: #3A2A1D` — crust-brown text, not near-black
- `--blue: #1F5D6E` — Sicilian majolica-tile teal, primary accent (headings, rules)
- `--brick: #A8441C` — wood-oven brick, secondary accent (small marks, borders)
- `--gold: #E4A62F` — lemon/citrus gold, tertiary highlight (hover, focus)

**Type**
- Display: **Fraunces** (high-contrast, warm old-style curves — the family name treated as hand-painted shop signage)
- Body: **Work Sans** — clean humanist sans, clearly distinct from Fraunces
- Body measure kept under 65ch; hero name set large with a centred signage lockup

**Layout**
Hero is a centred shopfront plaque (Italian shop signs are symmetrical, not off-axis), framed by a thin awning-stripe rule top and bottom. Below it, everything shifts to a left-aligned narrative column: a short founding paragraph, a genuine three-generation timeline (year markers are literal history, so a sequential device is earned here), a horizontal "display case" strip of four real specialties, and a visit card with hours/address plus a quote from the current owner.

```
──stripe──────────────────────
        Panificio
        Lo Cascio
   [short intro line]
──stripe──────────────────────
   [founding paragraph, ~60ch]

 1962 │ Salvatore opens the shop...
      │
 1989 │ Pino takes the oven...
      │
Today │ Rosa & Nino run counter...

 [cannoli][brioche][pane][sfincione]

 ┌ Visit us ─────┐   "quote"
 │ hours/address │   — Rosa
 └───────────────┘
```

**Principles**
- The one bold gesture is the signage lockup in the hero; everything after is quiet and disciplined.
- The only numbered/sequential device (the timeline) is used because the content genuinely is a sequence of three generations — not decoration.
- Palette is drawn from wheat, majolica blue, oven brick, and lemon — not the cream+terracotta default.
- Copy uses real names, streets, and pastries (sfincione, pane di casa) instead of generic bakery language.

# Review against the brief

Checked this against what a generic "artisan bakery" page would default to: cream background + terracotta accent + serif display (trait #1 in the tells list). I deliberately shifted the background warmer/more golden (wheat, not `#F4F1EA`) and made **blue**, not terracotta, the dominant accent, with brick reduced to a minor role — so the two don't collapse into the cliché combination. I also caught myself writing a meta line as `dal 1962 · Palermo` (a middle-dot tell) and rewrote it as a full sentence. No all-caps eyebrows, no arrow-suffixed links, and the timeline numbering is justified by real chronology rather than being a stock 01/02/03 device. Proceeding to build.

