# About Us — Panificio Cannizzaro

**Proposal:** I'll design this as the "about us" page for Panificio Cannizzaro, a fictional family bakery in Palermo's Kalsa quarter, run by three generations since 1962. Audience: locals and visitors who've found the shop (or its socials) and want the story behind the counter before they walk in — the page's job is to make them trust the ovens and want to visit. Proceeding straight through plan, review, and build below.

---

## Design plan

**Color** (grounded in Sicilian materials — plaster, volcanic stone, majolica tile, Bronte pistachio, not the generic cream/terracotta pairing)
| Name | Hex | Use |
|---|---|---|
| Grano | `#EDE4D3` | page background, warm plaster |
| Basalto | `#221D18` | body text, dark panels — warm near-black, not cold `#111` |
| Pistacchio | `#6B7B3F` | primary accent, awning stripe, rules |
| Maiolica | `#2F5F6B` | secondary accent, alternating stripe, links |
| Arancia | `#C9542C` | tertiary citrus pop, used sparingly (2–3 spots only) |
| Crema | `#FAF6EC` | panel fills, contrast against Grano |

**Type**
- Display: **Fraunces** — a serif with ball terminals and old-style figures, closer to hand-painted shop signage than a neutral editorial serif. Used for the wordmark and section heads, set large and tight.
- Body/UI: **Libre Franklin** — a sturdy grotesque with a full weight range, for the family story and long-form copy.

**Layout** — asymmetric and left-anchored throughout, like approaching the shop at an angle rather than facing a poster head-on.

```
HERO
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│▓▓▓▓ awning stripe (pistacchio/maiolica)│
│∪∪∪∪∪∪∪∪ scalloped fringe               │
│                                        │
│  Panificio Cannizzaro                 │
│  Bakers in Palermo's Kalsa quarter,   │
│  since 1962                           │
│  short lead line, left-aligned        │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘

STORY (two-column, sticky head / narrow text col)
│ Two rooms │ paragraph, ≤65ch
│ and a     │ paragraph, ≤65ch
│ wood oven │

TIMELINE (single vertical rule, years hung left)
1962 ─ line of text
1988 ─ line of text
2005 ─ line of text
Today ─ line of text

SHOWCASE (shelf: items on a baseline rule, staggered heights)
Cannolo   Brioche   Pasta di   Sfincione  Cassata  Granita
siciliano col tuppo mandorla
──────────────────────────────── (shelf edge)

FAMILY (three blocks, wax-seal roundel initials, no photo cards)
[SR] Salvatore & Rosa   [NT] Nino & Turi   [EM] Elena & Marco

VISIT (tile-bordered plaque, left-aligned)
Kalsa quarter, Palermo
Tue–Sun hours / closed Monday
```

Alignment: left throughout, ragged right — no centered hero, which is what most bakery/restaurant templates default to.

**Principles**
1. The awning is the one bold move — its stripe and scallop motif (a real Sicilian storefront device) bookends the page in hero and footer; everything else stays quiet.
2. The timeline uses real years because the family history genuinely is a sequence — not decorative 01/02/03 markers.
3. Copy stays plain and specific (ingredients, times, names) rather than promotional.
4. One motion moment: the awning unrolls on load, like a shutter opening for the day — thematically earned, not decorative.

---

## Review against the brief

Checked against the generic-AI defaults before building:
- Swapped the cliché cream-bg/serif/terracotta trio for a warmer plaster background and made orange a minor third accent behind pistachio and majolica-tile blue, so no single color reads as the "Claude orange" tell.
- Dropped the big-number/gradient hero in favor of a shopfront-signage hero, since the founding year is real content better shown as a family timeline than a stat.
- No all-caps eyebrows, no em-dash labels, no middle-dot meta strings, no trailing arrows on links, no monospace data labels.
- Showcase items are a shelf with a baseline rule and staggered heights, not identical rounded cards with drop shadows.
- Timeline numbering is the one "sequence" device — justified because the content is literally chronological.

---

## Code

```html
